        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Hadrian's Wall</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">122 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Begun in 122 in the reign of the emperor Hadrian; built by three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">Legio II Augusta</a>, Legio VI Victrix and Legio XX Valeria Victrix, with some members of the Roman fleet, and later garrisoned by auxiliary regiments</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A stone wall in its final version, with large ditches in front and behind, 80 Roman miles (73 modern miles, 117 kilometres) long, with forts, milecastles and turrets, and a western section first built of turf</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from Wallsend on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a> to Bowness-on-Solway; almost all the standing masonry was removed in early modern times, and many excavated forts are open to the public with museums nearby</span></div>
