    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hadrian ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s policy of expansion and defended the existing borders; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had no natural boundary, so a wall was built across the whole width of the island, begun in 122.</li><li>The wall was a physical barrier that slowed raiders and channelled movement through gates where it could be monitored and taxed, and it was also a statement of imperial power; scholars differ on how great the threat from the north was.</li><li>Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ended around 410, and the wall fell into ruin; its stone was reused for centuries, and much of what remains owes its preservation to the antiquarian John Clayton.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> alloy pan of the 2nd century, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> in 2003, is inscribed with the names of four forts on the western sector of the wall, followed by words that most likely read as 'Hadrian's Wall', though they may instead name a man called Aelius Draco.</p></aside>
    </section>

