    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">122</span><span class="rf-text">Construction of the wall began in the reign of the emperor Hadrian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">142</span><span class="rf-text">Work began on the turf-built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> in what is now central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, which briefly superseded Hadrian's Wall</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">164</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> and reoccupied Hadrian's Wall as the main defensive barrier</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">410</span><span class="rf-text">The estimated end of Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, after which the wall fell into ruin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">685</span><span class="rf-text">Stone reused from the wall was probably built into St Paul's Church at Monkwearmouth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> before its dedication stone was set</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1834</span><span class="rf-text">John Clayton began buying land along the wall to stop farmers taking its stones</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1987</span><span class="rf-text">The wall was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2005</span><span class="rf-text">It became part of the transnational Frontiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trajan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajan.jpg" alt="Trajan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traianus_Glyptothek_Munich_72.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hadrian's Wall was a defensive fortification of the Roman province of Britannia, begun in 122 in the reign of the emperor Hadrian. It was probably planned before Hadrian's visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 122. Restored sandstone fragments found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a>, dating from 118 or 119, record Hadrian's wish to keep the empire "intact", which had been imposed on him via "divine instruction". On his accession in 117 there was unrest and rebellion in Roman Britain and among the peoples of various conquered lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Judea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and Mauretania. These troubles may have influenced his plan for the wall, as well as his construction of frontier boundaries known as limes in other areas of the empire, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>Hadrian ended his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s policy of expanding the empire and focused on defending the current borders, which at the time included Britain. Like Augustus, he believed in exploiting natural boundaries such as rivers, for example the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Britain had no natural boundary that could divide the province controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from the Celtic tribes in the north. The route lay slightly north of the Stanegate, an important Roman road built several decades earlier to link two forts that guarded river crossings: Corstopitum (Corbridge) on the Tyne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Luguvalium</a> (Carlisle) on the Eden.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wall ran for 80 Roman miles, equivalent to 73 modern miles or 117 kilometres, from Segedunum at Wallsend on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>, via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> and Kirkandrews-on-Eden, to the shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway Firth</a>, ending a short but unknown distance west of Bowness-on-Solway. In its final version it was a stone wall with large ditches in front and behind, and part of the central section follows natural cliffs on an escarpment of the Whin Sill rock formation. Along it stood large forts, smaller milecastles and, every third of a mile, a watch-tower turret that also gave shelter and living accommodation to troops. The system of milecastles and turrets continued along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> coast as far as Risehow, south of Maryport.</p><p>Just south of the wall lay the Vallum, a deep ditch-like construction with a mound running along each side of it. On the berm in front of the wall were pits known as cippi, which held branches or small tree trunks entangled with sharpened branches, which might be thought of as the Roman equivalent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbed-wire">barbed wire</a>. Movement was channelled through the gates in the wall, where it could be monitored, prevented or permitted as appropriate, and taxed. There is some evidence that the wall was originally covered in plaster and whitewashed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septimius-severus.jpg" alt="Septimius Severus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Construction began in 122, with an alternating series of forts, each housing 600 men, and manned milecastles, operated by "between 12 and 20 men". It took six years to build most of the wall, with the work coming from three legions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">Legio II Augusta</a>, Legio VI Victrix and Legio XX Valeria Victrix, totalling 15,000 soldiers, plus some members of the Roman fleet. Some of the soldiers would have trained as surveyors, engineers, masons and carpenters. Not long after construction began, the planned thickness of 10 feet was reduced to about 8 feet, or less depending on the terrain. R. G. Collingwood cited evidence for a Broad Wall and a Narrow Wall and argued that the plans possibly changed because of a lack of resources.</p><p>From Milecastle 49 to Bowness-on-Solway the wall was originally built of turf, possibly because limestone was absent. This turf wall was 6 metres wide at its base and about 3.66 metres high. It was later demolished and replaced in stone, in two phases: the first during Hadrian's reign, the second after the reoccupation that followed the abandonment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>, though it has also been suggested that the second took place under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>. The date of the Vallum was disputed. Collingwood asserted in 1930 that it was built before the wall in its final form, but research in 1936 found that it avoided one of the wall's milecastles, which supported a simultaneous construction.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Hadrian's Wall&#8221; cites where it covers Construction, Turf wall, Standards:</p><ul><li>David J Breeze, John Collingwood Bruce's Handbook to the Roman Wall (Society of Antiquaries of Newcastle upon Tyne, 1934)</li><li>David J. Breeze, John Collingwood Bruce's Handbook to the Roman Wall (Society of Antiquaries of Newcastle upon Tyne, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china.jpg" alt="Great Wall of China" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Severin.stalder, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Almost 10,000 soldiers are thought to have been stationed on the wall once it was fully manned, drawn not from the legions that built it but from regiments of auxiliary infantry and cavalry from the provinces. David Breeze holds that the troops in the forts had the primary duty of defence, while those in the milecastles and turrets were responsible for frontier control. Scholars differ on the purpose of the wall. Its primary purpose was probably as a physical barrier to slow raiders intent on taking animals, treasure or slaves; it also kept people within the province. Neil Faulkner called it "as much a propaganda statement as a functional facility". In recent years some scholars have questioned how much of a threat the inhabitants of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> presented and whether a fixed line of defences gave any economic advantage.</p><p>The wall probably cut across a coherent cultural area. To the south, Roman-style settlements appear in the early 2nd century, very shortly after it was built. To the north a large area of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and the Northumbrian coastal plain lost its monumental building tradition, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> may have cleared a zone of its population. Roman coins and pottery generally did not cross the wall, which seems to have been an effective barrier to trade, though exchange may have been managed at a few crossing points. Breeze and B. Dobson suggested that Hadrian may have been influenced by accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China</a>; Duncan B. Campbell argued that a long tradition of wall-building in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world gave a precedent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius.jpg" alt="Antoninus Pius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoninus_Pius_bust_S2436_ancient_agora_museum_Athens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jebulon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Hadrian's death in 138, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> left the wall occupied in a support role and began building the turf-built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> about 160 kilometres north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> abandoned that wall and reoccupied Hadrian's Wall in 164. In 208–211 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> again tried to conquer Caledonia and temporarily reoccupied the Antonine Wall; the campaign ended inconclusively and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> withdrew to Hadrian's Wall. By 410, the estimated end of Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the Roman administration and its legions were gone, though archaeologists have found that some parts of the wall stayed occupied well into the 5th century. The wall fell into ruin and its stone was reused in local buildings; some of it was built into St Paul's Church at Monkwearmouth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> was a monk.</p><p>Bede, who died in 735, wrote that the wall was 8 feet broad and 12 feet high, but he appears to have believed that the Vallum was a rampart built by Severus. Almost all the standing masonry was removed in early modern times for local roads and farmhouses, and in the 18th century General Wade used long sections for the military road built to crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a>. The antiquarian John Clayton, from 1834, bought land along the wall to stop farmers taking its stones, excavated forts and milecastles, and had sections restored to about seven courses. After his death the estate was lost to gambling, and the National Trust began acquiring the land. In 2021 workers found a previously undiscovered 3-metre section in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bede"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bede.jpg" alt="Bede" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E-codices_bke-0047_001v_medium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">e-codices.unifr.ch, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hadrian's Wall is the largest Roman archaeological feature in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and is regarded as a British cultural icon. It lies entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and has never formed the Anglo-Scottish border, though it is sometimes loosely described as such. It was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1987, and in 2005 it became part of the transnational Frontiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> site, which also includes sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Hadrian's Wall Path, a National Trail, opened in 2003 and follows the line of the wall.</p><p>In the Roman period the wall seems to have been called the vallum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> vessels such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> Moorlands Pan, the Rudge Cup, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> Skillet and the Berlanga Cup bear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> names of its forts, with a depiction of a battlemented wall beneath, and are thought to have been souvenirs valued by owners who might have been veterans of the auxiliary units there. John Speed called the wall the "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> Wall" and William Matthew's 1610 map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> named it "Severus' Wall", following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s name for the Vallum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudyard-kipling">Rudyard Kipling</a>'s Puck of Pook's Hill (1906) contributed to the popular image of the "Great Pict Wall", and W. H. Auden wrote a 1937 radio script and the poem "Roman Wall Blues" in the voice of a lonely Roman soldier stationed there.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hadrian's Wall, begun in 122, was a Roman fortification 80 Roman miles long across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with forts, milecastles and turrets. It was briefly superseded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>, fell into ruin after Roman rule ended and was quarried for centuries, and much of what survives owes its preservation to John Clayton.</em></p>

